</div><includeonly>{{#if:{{{text|}}}||[[Category:Pages with template errors]]}}{{#switch:{{{type|}}}| = | warning = | #default = [[Category:Pages with template errors]]}}</includeonly></onlyinclude><!--
  Two registers. {{note|text=...}} is an aside: here is something worth knowing on the way past.
  {{note|type=warning|text=...}} is something the reader has to act on, and it says so with the
  warning colours and a mark of its own. Fourteen of the sixteen uses in this documentation are
  asides, which is why the two that are not have to look different: a box that reads the same
  either way teaches the reader to skim it, and the two that matter are then the two skimmed.

  The text is a named parameter, and that is what closes the trap this template used to carry.
  An unnamed argument holding "=" is parsed as a named one, so {{note|A stylesheet arrives as
  text/css; charset=UTF-8 ...}} reached the template with no text at all and drew the placeholder
  below, across nine files, with every check green. Named, the first "=" is the one that splits
  the name from the value and every "=" after it is part of the text. A "|" still splits, but a
  link's own is safe: [[...|...]] is balanced and the parser does not read through it.

  A call that names nothing -- {{note|Some text}} -- therefore leaves text unset, draws the
  placeholder, and files into Category:Pages with template errors rather than looking right. So
  does a type that is neither empty nor "warning": {{note|type=warn|text=...}} is a warning the
  reader never sees. Adding a register means adding it to the switch as well as to the class and
  the icon above.

  WikvenFailOnCategories in .wikven.yaml names the category, so a build that fills it stops and
  says which page did. includeonly keeps this page itself out of it; it is outside onlyinclude
  so that no page carries it.
-->
